= Digital organism =

A '''digital organism''' is a self-replicating executable program used as an experimental model of evolution. Digital organisms contain heritable instruction sequences, reproduce within a computational environment, acquire mutations, compete for limited resources, and can evolve by natural selection.

The term “organism” is operational and model-based. Digital organisms are not biological cells: they do not contain DNA, proteins, membranes, or biochemical metabolism. Their scientific value comes from implementing core evolutionary processes in a system whose complete state can be recorded and experimentally manipulated.

[[Artificial life]] research uses digital organisms to study adaptation, mutation, selection, ecological interaction, complexity, and open-ended evolution.

== Historical development ==

Early experiments with self-reproducing programs preceded modern digital-evolution platforms, but Thomas Ray's '''Tierra''' became a major landmark. Ray described a computational environment in which machine-code-like programs copied themselves, mutated, competed for memory and processor time, and evolved ecological interactions.<ref name="ray">[https://tomray.me/pubs/ Ray, T. S. (1991), “An Approach to the Synthesis of Life,” in ''Artificial Life II'', pp. 371–408.]</ref>

'''Avida''' later developed a more controlled experimental platform. Each Avida organism executes a digital genome on a virtual CPU, replicates with mutation, and can receive computational resources for performing specified tasks. Ofria and Wilke describe Avida explicitly as a platform for experiments with self-replicating and evolving computer programs.<ref name="avida">[https://doi.org/10.1162/106454604773563612 Ofria, C. and Wilke, C. O. (2004), “Avida: A Software Platform for Research in Computational Evolutionary Biology,” ''Artificial Life'' 10(2), 191–229.]</ref>

Digital evolution has been used for controlled tests of evolutionary hypotheses. Lenski and colleagues used Avida to analyze the stepwise evolutionary origin of a complex logic function, reconstructing lineages and testing intermediate genotypes.<ref name="lenski">[https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01568 Lenski, R. E. et al. (2003), “The evolutionary origin of complex features,” ''Nature'' 423, 139–144.]</ref>

== Digital genome ==

A digital genome is usually a sequence of executable instructions,

<math>g=(i_1,i_2,\ldots,i_L).</math>

An interpreter or virtual CPU maps the genome and environmental state to behavior:

<math>\phi=Exec(g,E).</math>

The resulting phenotype can include:

* replication time;
* resource acquisition;
* task performance;
* interactions with other organisms;
* movement through digital space;
* production of offspring.

Unlike a static bit string in a conventional genetic algorithm, a digital organism can execute an algorithm that controls its own replication.

== Replication and inheritance ==

Reproduction creates a descendant genome <math>g'</math> from parent <math>g</math>:

<math>g'=R(g,\xi),</math>

where <math>\xi</math> represents mutation events.

Depending on the platform, mutations can include:

* substitutions;
* insertions;
* deletions;
* copy errors;
* instruction changes during division.

Inheritance is digital but not necessarily error-free.

A system becomes evolutionarily interesting when replication creates heritable variation and variants differ in reproductive success.

== Natural selection ==

Let genotype <math>i</math> have frequency <math>x_i</math> and fitness <math>f_i</math>. A deterministic approximation is

<math>\dot{x}_i=x_i(f_i-\bar f),</math>

where

<math>\bar f=\sum_j x_jf_j.</math>

Actual digital populations are finite and therefore also experience stochastic drift.

Fitness is environment-dependent. A genome rewarded for one computational function can lose its advantage if the reward structure changes.

There is no context-free digital fitness.

== Resource competition ==

Digital environments can impose scarcity through:

* processor time;
* memory;
* spatial sites;
* energy-like resource counters;
* task-dependent rewards.

If a total computational budget is <math>C</math>, allocations can satisfy

<math>\sum_i c_i\leq C.</math>

Scarcity links behavior to reproductive success and allows ecological interaction.

Without resource constraints, every replicator may expand independently and selection pressures can be substantially different.

== Tierra ==

Tierra placed replicating programs into a shared computational environment.

Important outcomes included:

* competition for memory and execution time;
* changes in genome length;
* parasite-like programs that exploited replication routines of other organisms;
* higher-order ecological responses.

These interactions were not simply labels assigned by the researcher. They emerged from executable behavior under the rules of the environment.

The ecological analogy should nevertheless remain precise: a Tierra parasite is a digital strategy exploiting another program's machinery, not a biochemical parasite.

== Avida ==

Avida isolates organisms more strongly than Tierra and provides detailed experimental control.

An Avida organism typically has:

* a genome;
* virtual registers and stacks;
* an instruction pointer;
* an execution history;
* a parent lineage;
* reproductive output.

Researchers can configure mutation rates, population size, spatial structure, task rewards, and environmental changes.

Complete ancestry and genotype histories allow experiments that are impossible or difficult in biological populations.

== Experimental evolution ==

Digital organisms support replicated evolutionary experiments.

A general design can be:

# initialize many populations from the same ancestor;
# assign treatment and control environments;
# evolve populations for a fixed number of updates or generations;
# archive genomes through time;
# measure fitness and phenotype;
# replay ancestral and evolved genotypes;
# test causal mutations.

Because the experimenter controls the environment, competing hypotheses can often be tested by direct intervention.

== Lineage reconstruction ==

For a lineage

<math>L=(g_0,g_1,\ldots,g_n),</math>

every inherited change can be retained.

Researchers can construct intermediate genotypes and ask which mutation changed a phenotype.

This permits causal decomposition of historical pathways.

Lenski et al.'s digital-evolution work used this ability to study how a complex function arose through mutations whose effects depended on prior genetic context.<ref name="lenski" />

== Epistasis ==

The effect of one mutation can depend on another.

For mutations <math>A</math> and <math>B</math>, one simple additive-scale interaction is

<math>\epsilon=W_{AB}-W_A-W_B+W_0.</math>

Nonzero <math>\epsilon</math> indicates departure from additivity on that scale.

Digital systems can map such interactions across large numbers of precisely known genotypes.

== Neutral evolution ==

Different genomes can have similar or identical measured fitness.

A population can therefore move across a neutral network in genotype space.

Neutral changes can alter which beneficial or harmful mutations are accessible later, affecting evolvability without producing an immediate fitness increase.

== Ecology and coevolution ==

Digital populations can exhibit:

* competition;
* parasitism;
* mutual dependence;
* frequency-dependent selection;
* spatial structure;
* arms races.

To call an interaction ecological, the mechanism should be defined operationally. For example, a parasite should obtain a reproductive advantage by exploiting another organism's resources or functions, not merely by belonging to a category named “parasite.”

== Complexity ==

Digital evolution can produce more complex behavior, but complexity requires an explicit metric.

Possible measures include:

* genome length;
* number of essential instructions;
* algorithmic task complexity;
* dependency structure;
* phenotypic repertoire;
* information required to specify an adaptation.

Genome length alone is weak because genomes can accumulate neutral or redundant code.

A claim that complexity increased should state which quantity increased and why it matters.

== Open-ended evolution ==

'''Open-ended evolution''' refers to sustained production of evolutionary novelty rather than convergence on a fixed set of solutions.

Digital systems commonly face limits such as:

* finite instruction sets;
* fixed task rewards;
* bounded environments;
* resource ceilings;
* repetitive ecological states.

Long runtimes alone do not demonstrate open-endedness.

Researchers need measures of novelty, diversity, ecological change, and continued creation of new adaptive opportunities.

== Digital organisms and genetic algorithms ==

A genetic algorithm usually treats candidate solutions as data structures evaluated by an external fitness function.

A digital organism is normally executable and carries out behavior—including replication—inside its environment.

The boundary is not absolute, but self-replication and autonomous execution are central to the digital-organism concept.

== Digital organisms and computer viruses ==

Computer viruses also self-replicate, but their usual purpose and environment differ.

A virus is generally studied as software that propagates through real computer systems, often without authorization. Digital organisms are typically confined to controlled experimental environments designed to study evolution.

Shared technical properties do not make the research categories identical.

== Digital organisms and AI agents ==

An [[Intelligent agent]] can optimize decisions without reproducing or evolving.

A digital organism can reproduce and evolve while having little capability that would ordinarily be called intelligence.

Evolution and intelligence are therefore independent dimensions.

Evolutionary processes can, however, be used to evolve agent controllers.

== Relation to biological evolution ==

Digital organisms can implement genuine abstract conditions for Darwinian evolution:

* reproduction;
* heredity;
* variation;
* differential reproductive success.

What they do not automatically reproduce are the full constraints of biological life.

Digital systems may omit or simplify:

* molecular chemistry;
* development;
* multicellularity;
* biomechanics;
* environmental physics;
* horizontal gene transfer;
* ecological diversity;
* energetic metabolism.

A digital result supports a general biological claim only when the mechanism of interest survives those differences.

== Life status ==

Whether a digital organism should literally be called “alive” depends on the definition of life.

Digital organisms can satisfy operational definitions based on replication and evolution but fail definitions requiring biochemical metabolism, cellular organization, or autonomous material self-maintenance.

[[Autopoiesis]] supplies a different criterion: a system must recursively produce the components and boundary that constitute its organization. Most conventional digital organisms rely on externally supplied hardware, virtual-machine rules, and execution infrastructure and therefore should not be assumed autopoietic.

== Consciousness ==

Evolution, replication, adaptation, and behavioral complexity do not establish subjective experience.

There is no general inference

<pre>
evolved digital behavior → consciousness
</pre>

without an independently justified theory and discriminating evidence.

Digital-organism experiments should therefore report measurable computational and evolutionary properties without anthropomorphic inflation.

== Methodological strengths ==

Digital evolution offers unusually strong experimental access:

* exact state recording;
* complete genealogy;
* reproducible starting conditions;
* controlled mutation;
* rapid generation time;
* destructive genotype tests;
* counterfactual replay;
* replicated populations.

These strengths make digital organisms valuable for mechanism testing.

== Methodological limitations ==

The same explicit rules that make digital systems tractable can constrain their conclusions.

Important risks include:

* researcher-designed reward functions;
* unrealistic mutation operators;
* finite task vocabularies;
* artificial resource accounting;
* dependence on one instruction set;
* hidden software bugs;
* overgeneralization from model to biology.

A robust study states which conclusions concern the digital system and which are proposed to generalize beyond it.

== Reproducibility ==

A reproducible digital-evolution study should report:

* simulator version and source code;
* random seeds;
* ancestral genome;
* instruction set;
* mutation operators and rates;
* population structure;
* resource and reward schedules;
* termination criteria;
* analysis scripts.

Because every event is computable, failure to preserve this information is avoidable loss of scientific evidence.

== Related concepts ==

Digital organisms connect [[Related to::Artificial life|artificial life]], [[Related to::Genetic inheritance|genetic inheritance]], [[Related to::Adaptation (systems)|adaptation]], [[Related to::Computation|computation]], and [[Related to::Autopoiesis|autopoiesis]] as a contrasting autonomy criterion. They also inform evolution and [[Emergence]], but they are not automatically biological organisms, intelligent agents, or conscious systems.

